window.docContents[50].resolve({"/Tier-1___-statements/Theorem-files/Computational.Theorems.Main/#file-Computational___Theorems___Main":{"contents":"Paper › Computational/Theorems › Main.leanTier 1 · theorem file\n\nTier 1 · theorem file used by the computational proof 2 headline theorems 111 lines trust banner B1, trusted: the statement source ↗Read in full. This file states headline theorems. Proofs are cut after := and shown as ⋯; Lean checks them.This file is in the non-default library CliElfLib (its theorems need the ELF built from source).\n\nCited in the paper* Theorem 2 (Binary security, simplified), §5.2.3: libsignal_cli_pq_secure_elf_binary* Lemma 4 (Machine code refines the model), §C.3: libsignal_cli_pq_binary_correct\n\n\n\nLemma 4 and Main Theorem: Computational Correctness and Security of the libsignal-cli BinaryTrusted: the statement. Built by lake build CliElfLib. Each theorem below states an ELF-direct proposition from Computational/Specifications/ or LibsignalBinary/Specifications/ on the SHA-256-pinned AArch64 binary cliElfBytes (LibsignalBinary/Specifications/SignalSession/CliElf.lean) and is proved by a corresponding *_proof theorem in Computational/Proofs/. See Computational/docs/TRUSTED_BASE.md for the trusted definitions and kernel axioms of each theorem, and Computational/docs/DEVIATIONS.md for the specification deviations referenced below.Lemmas 1–3 (Computational/Theorems/Lemmas.lean), ELF-direct Lemma 4, and freedom from .OutOfMemory in PQXDH mode imply the KEM-generic security theorem libsignal_cli_pq_secure_elf_binary_any_kem (main_of_lemmas, Computational/Proofs/LibsignalCli/MainOfLemmas.lean), which combined with the concrete Round-3 CRYSTALS-Kyber-1024 δ_kem bound kyber1024_kem_delta_correct (Computational/Theorems/Lemmas.lean, DEV-PQ21) yields the Main Theorem libsignal_cli_pq_secure_elf_binary; for cliElfBytes, .OutOfMemory freedom is discharged unconditionally under the 1 GiB dynamic-mapping budget.* Result* Theorem* Statement* Proof* Lemma 4* libsignal_cli_pq_binary_correct* CliPqxdhBinaryCorrect cliElfBytes (LibsignalBinary/Specifications/SignalSession/CliBinaryPq.lean)* Computational/Proofs/LibsignalCli/ElfHeadlinePq.lean* KEM-generic CLI security (intermediate)* libsignal_cli_pq_secure_elf_binary_any_kem* CliPqxdhBinaryCorrectAndSecureAnyKem cliElfBytes (Computational/Specifications/LibsignalCli/StatementPqElfAnyKem.lean)* Computational/Proofs/LibsignalCli/ElfHeadlinePq.lean* Main Theorem* libsignal_cli_pq_secure_elf_binary* CliPqxdhBinaryCorrectAndSecure cliElfBytes (Computational/Specifications/LibsignalCli/StatementPqElf.lean)* Computational/Proofs/LibsignalCli/ElfHeadlinePq.leanTrusted computing base. The statements reference cliElfBytes directly without any library placement (Obj.MultiPlacement / libsignalSessionLib): every instruction except the 18 Rust panic entry points (DEV-E7) executes from plain loaded ELF memory (ElfImage.loadState, zero initial memory patches), including aes and spqr CPU-feature probes against the pinned auxv (AT_HWCAP = 0x1b, AT_HWCAP2 = 0, AT_RANDOM = 0^16). The .symtab trust point is that the ELF .symtab labels the 18 panic entry points and 5 SampleNTT entries accurately (resolveCliElfAddrs; Computational/docs/TRUSTED_BASE.md).\n\n\n\ntheorem libsignal_cli_pq_binary_correctL58–68 ↗#Lemma 4 (binary refinement, ELF-direct) (CliPqxdhBinaryCorrect cliElfBytes, LibsignalBinary/Specifications/SignalSession/CliBinaryPq.lean). cliElfBytes parses as an AArch64 ELF image, resolves the 23 .symtab addresses (18 panic + 5 SampleNTT) via resolveCliElfAddrs, and for all model extras X, the ELF-direct process cliRunElf (starting from plain ElfImage.loadState) satisfies CliSpecPqxdh and CliSpecPqxdhNoMatch (DEV-CLI11) at the closed primitives realPEParams (SPQR V1) except on runs that reach .OutOfMemory (CliReachesOOMElf, DEV-CLI15) or a non-terminating libcrux_ml_kem SampleNTT matrix seed (CliReachesSampleNttStuckElf, DEV-CLI22).\n\n\n\ntheorem libsignal_cli_pq_secure_elf_binary_any_kemL76–90 ↗#KEM-generic binary correctness and security (intermediate lemma) (CliPqxdhBinaryCorrectAndSecureAnyKem cliElfBytes, Computational/Specifications/LibsignalCli/StatementPqElfAnyKem.lean). cliElfBytes parses as an AArch64 ELF image, resolves the 23 .symtab addresses via resolveCliElfAddrs, satisfies CliPqxdhElfCorrect (allowing .OutOfMemory, DEV-CLI15, and divergent SampleNTT seeds, DEV-CLI22), and for every setup S and plaintext length ℓ satisfies CliPqxdhElfSecureAnyKem on cliRunElf elf addrs (starting from plain ElfImage.loadState) at the closed primitives realPEParams, the concrete Curve25519 prime-order x-only subgroup (X25519Pt, x25519Dh, x25519Delta = 1 / 2 ^ 250), and the concrete Curve25519/XEdDSA unreached operations realUnreachedPrims kemKeyGen for every valid, collision-bounded, δ_kem-correct Kyber-1024 kemKeyGen (with no .OutOfMemory premise, and additive excused-abort term on divergent SampleNTT seeds CliReachesSampleNttStuckElf, DEV-CLI22).\n\ntheorem libsignal_cli_pq_secure_elf_binaryL92–109 ↗#Main Theorem (binary correctness and security, ELF-direct, concrete Kyber-1024) (CliPqxdhBinaryCorrectAndSecure cliElfBytes, Computational/Specifications/LibsignalCli/StatementPqElf.lean). cliElfBytes parses as an AArch64 ELF image, resolves the 23 .symtab addresses via resolveCliElfAddrs, satisfies CliPqxdhElfCorrect (allowing .OutOfMemory, DEV-CLI15, and divergent SampleNTT seeds, DEV-CLI22), and for every setup S and plaintext length ℓ satisfies CliPqxdhElfSecure on cliRunElf elf addrs (starting from plain ElfImage.loadState) at the closed primitives realPEParams, the concrete Curve25519 prime-order x-only subgroup (X25519Pt, x25519Dh, x25519Delta = 1 / 2 ^ 250), and the concrete Curve25519/XEdDSA and round-3 CRYSTALS-Kyber-1024 unreached operations realUnreachedPrims CryptoGames.MLKEM.kyber1024KemKeyGen with concrete decapsulation failure parameter δ_kem = ε + 1 / 2 ^ 112 under KyberNoiseGap ε (DEV-PQ21; key-generation wire lengths (1568, 3168) proved unconditionally, public-key collision bound ≤ 1 / 2 ^ 255 retained as a premise, Clause 2 conditional on KeyIndRoGap, DEV-CLI16, with no .OutOfMemory premise and additive excused-abort term on divergent SampleNTT seeds CliReachesSampleNttStuckElf, DEV-CLI22).\n\n\n\n","context":"AlphaProof Code: Verifying Signal’s Security at the Machine-Code Level","header":"Computational/Theorems/Main.lean","id":"/Tier-1___-statements/Theorem-files/Computational.Theorems.Main/#file-Computational___Theorems___Main"},"/Tier-2___-definitions/Computational___Specifications___/Computational.Specifications.Session.ACD19.SM.Theorem8And12Statements/#file-Computational___Specifications___Session___ACD19___SM___Theorem8And12Statements":{"contents":"Paper › Computational/Specifications/Session/ACD19/SM › Theorem8And12Statements.leanTier 2 · definitions\n\nTier 2 · definitions used by the computational proof 2 headline theorems depend on it 13 of 78 lines trusted trust banner B2, trusted: definitions used by the statement source ↗Read the definitions shown. Only the declarations that the headline statements use are shown, with the namespace, section, open and variable commands needed to read them. The collapsed runs are theorems, which Lean checks, and definitions that no headline statement uses.\n\nTheorems 8 and 12: authenticity and privacy of the Fig. 8 SM scheme (statements)Trusted: definitions used by the statement. The theorems of Computational/Theorems/ use healEpoch and SM.specDF, the only definitions in this file. SchemeAdversary, Theorem8 and Theorem12 are in Computational/Proofs/SpecLemmas/Session/ACD19/SM/Theorem8And12Statements.lean, checked by Lean. See Computational/docs/TRUSTED_BASE.md §7.In ACD19 (Alwen, Coretti, Dodis, ePrint 2018/1037, §5.3.2–5.3.3, Theorems 8 and 12), the Fig. 8 Double Ratchet secure-messaging scheme (LibsignalBinary/Specifications/CryptoGames/SM/Construction.lean) achieves authenticity and privacy for Δ_SM = 2 + Δ_CKA from a CKA scheme, a root PRF-PRNG, and an FS-AEAD scheme:* Adv^{auth}_{t*_L,t*}(A) ≤ Adv^{CKA}(R₁ A) + Adv^{PP}(R₂ A) + Σ_{j<q} Adv^{fs-aead}(R₃,j A),* `Adv^{priv}_{t*_L,t*}(A) ≤ 2·Adv^{CKA}(R₁ A) + 2·Adv^{PP}(R₂ A) + Adv^{fs-aead}(R₃ A)* 2·Adv^{fs-aead}(R₃' A)`,for q-query adversaries A, attacked epoch t*, last prior leak t*_L, and CKA healing epoch t_heal = t*_L + 2 (healEpoch). Theorem8 and Theorem12 parameterize over the reductions R₁, R₂, R₃, instantiated and proved in Computational/Proofs/CryptoGames/SM/.Authenticity is a winning probability while CKA and PRF-PRNG advantages are real/ideal distances, so each hop costs 1× advantage, and R₃,j attacks the j-th epoch-t* FS-AEAD session and outputs a coin. Privacy is a bias (2·|Pr − 1/2|), so each distance hop costs 2× advantage (DEV-SM20) and the final FS-AEAD hop costs Adv(R₃) + 2·Adv(R₃'), where R₃' outputs a coin on forgery (DEV-SM25); ACD19 writes factor 1 on both bounds and omits the forgery term in Lemma 15.\n\n\n\nabbrev specDFL53–56 ↗#Six-oracle DeliverForge SM specification extending SM.spec with (Party × C × C) →ₒ Option (RecvResult Msg) for distinct ciphertexts sharing wire bytes.\n\n\n\ndef healEpochL64–72 ↗#CKA challenge (healing) epoch t_heal = t*_L + 2, or 0 when t*_L = −∞.DEV-SM27 (formalization): t*_L = −∞. ACD19 (§5.3.2, footnote 13) omits the case with no leak before t*, where the root RNG starts uncorrupted and no CKA step is needed. Setting healEpoch none = 0 places the CKA challenge at epoch 0, which has no challenge and thus zero advantage for every adversary.\n\n\n\n","context":"AlphaProof Code: Verifying Signal’s Security at the Machine-Code Level","header":"Computational/Specifications/Session/ACD19/SM/Theorem8And12Statements.lean","id":"/Tier-2___-definitions/Computational___Specifications___/Computational.Specifications.Session.ACD19.SM.Theorem8And12Statements/#file-Computational___Specifications___Session___ACD19___SM___Theorem8And12Statements"},"/Tier-2___-definitions/LibsignalBinary___Specifications___/LibsignalBinary.Specifications.RecordCodecs.Proto/#file-LibsignalBinary___Specifications___RecordCodecs___Proto":{"contents":"Paper › LibsignalBinary/Specifications/RecordCodecs › Proto.leanTier 2 · definitions\n\nTier 2 · definitions used by the computational proof and symbolic proof 7 headline theorems depend on it 56 of 256 lines trusted trust banner B3, trusted in part source ↗Read the definitions shown. Only the declarations that the headline statements use are shown, with the namespace, section, open and variable commands needed to read them. The collapsed runs are theorems, which Lean checks, and definitions that no headline statement uses.\n\nDeclarations on this page (10)def le64Bytesdef encodeProto3BytesFielddef encodeProto3Uint32Fielddef encodeProto3Fixed64Fieldstructure PreKeyRecordStructuredef encodestructure SignedPreKeyRecordStructuredef encodestructure IdentityKeyPairStructuredef encode\n\nProtobuf Record Codecs for Identity and Pre-Key StoresTrusted in part. SignalSession/PqDir.lean (encodeDir) encodes identity and pre-key store files with IdentityKeyPairStructure, SignedPreKeyRecordStructure, and PreKeyRecordStructure (encode) via encodeProto3BytesField, encodeProto3Uint32Field, encodeProto3Fixed64Field, and le64Bytes; the decoders (empty, mergeField, decode, decodeFieldsLoop, skipUnknown, mergeFixed64, le64) and record wrappers (PreKeyRecord, SignedPreKeyRecord, KyberPreKeyRecord, deserialize, serialize) are checked by Lean. See Computational/docs/TRUSTED_BASE.md.Models prost 0.14.4's proto3 encoders (tag-ordered fields, omitting default 0 / empty values) and decoders (Message::merge from default with last-write-wins field merging and unknown-field skipping up to recursion limit 100) for storage.proto messages.\n\n\n\n3 declarations not shown (lines 38–64): theorems, which Lean checks, or definitions that no headline statement usesCryptoVerification.RecordCodecs.decodeFieldsLoop, CryptoVerification.RecordCodecs.skipUnknown, CryptoVerification.RecordCodecs.le64\n\ndef le64BytesL66–69 ↗#BufMut::put_u64_le: the 8 little-endian bytes of v.\n\n1 declaration not shown (lines 71–78): theorems, which Lean checks, or definitions that no headline statement usesCryptoVerification.RecordCodecs.mergeFixed64\n\nproto3 field encoders (omit the default value, D/field/scalar.rs:119–126)\n\ndef encodeProto3BytesFieldL82–85 ↗#A proto3 bytes field: nothing when empty, else bytes::encode(tag, value) (P/encoding.rs:691–695).\n\ndef encodeProto3Uint32FieldL87–89 ↗#A proto3 uint32 field: nothing when 0, else uint32::encode(tag, value).\n\ndef encodeProto3Fixed64FieldL91–93 ↗#A proto3 fixed64 field: nothing when 0, else the key and put_u64_le(value).\n\nPreKeyRecordStructure (storage.proto:76–80)\n\nstructure PreKeyRecordStructureL97–106 ↗#message PreKeyRecordStructure { uint32 id = 1; bytes public_key = 2; bytes private_key = 3; } (prost struct proto::storage::PreKeyRecordStructure).\n\n\n\n3 declarations not shown (lines 110–124): theorems, which Lean checks, or definitions that no headline statement usesCryptoVerification.RecordCodecs.PreKeyRecordStructure.empty, CryptoVerification.RecordCodecs.PreKeyRecordStructure.mergeField, CryptoVerification.RecordCodecs.PreKeyRecordStructure.decode\n\ndef encodeL126–128 ↗#<PreKeyRecordStructure as Message>::encode_to_vec.\n\n\n\nSignedPreKeyRecordStructure (storage.proto:82–88)\n\nstructure SignedPreKeyRecordStructureL134–148 ↗#message SignedPreKeyRecordStructure { uint32 id = 1; bytes public_key = 2; bytes private_key = 3; bytes signature = 4; fixed64 timestamp = 5; }. Both SignedPreKeyRecord (X25519 keys) and KyberPreKeyRecord (KEM keys) store one.\n\n\n\n3 declarations not shown (lines 152–168): theorems, which Lean checks, or definitions that no headline statement usesCryptoVerification.RecordCodecs.SignedPreKeyRecordStructure.empty, CryptoVerification.RecordCodecs.SignedPreKeyRecordStructure.mergeField, CryptoVerification.RecordCodecs.SignedPreKeyRecordStructure.decode\n\ndef encodeL170–173 ↗#<SignedPreKeyRecordStructure as Message>::encode_to_vec.\n\n\n\nIdentityKeyPairStructure (storage.proto:90–93)\n\nstructure IdentityKeyPairStructureL179–185 ↗#message IdentityKeyPairStructure { bytes public_key = 1; bytes private_key = 2; }.\n\n\n\n3 declarations not shown (lines 189–202): theorems, which Lean checks, or definitions that no headline statement usesCryptoVerification.RecordCodecs.IdentityKeyPairStructure.empty, CryptoVerification.RecordCodecs.IdentityKeyPairStructure.mergeField, CryptoVerification.RecordCodecs.IdentityKeyPairStructure.decode\n\ndef encodeL204–206 ↗#<IdentityKeyPairStructure as Message>::encode_to_vec.\n\n\n\n6 declarations not shown (lines 212–254): theorems, which Lean checks, or definitions that no headline statement usesCryptoVerification.RecordCodecs.PreKeyRecord.preKey, CryptoVerification.RecordCodecs.PreKeyRecord, CryptoVerification.RecordCodecs.PreKeyRecord.deserialize, CryptoVerification.RecordCodecs.SignedPreKeyRecord, CryptoVerification.RecordCodecs.SignedPreKeyRecord.signedPreKey, CryptoVerification.RecordCodecs.SignedPreKeyRecord.deserialize, CryptoVerification.RecordCodecs.KyberPreKeyRecord.signedPreKey, CryptoVerification.RecordCodecs.KyberPreKeyRecord, CryptoVerification.RecordCodecs.KyberPreKeyRecord.deserialize\n\n\n\n","context":"AlphaProof Code: Verifying Signal’s Security at the Machine-Code Level","header":"LibsignalBinary/Specifications/RecordCodecs/Proto.lean","id":"/Tier-2___-definitions/LibsignalBinary___Specifications___/LibsignalBinary.Specifications.RecordCodecs.Proto/#file-LibsignalBinary___Specifications___RecordCodecs___Proto"}});